module gunbc.runner.runner_gpu_container_toolchain

// THE GPU CONTAINER TOOLCHAIN AS A FLEET CAPABILITY: declared per host in
// gunbc.runner.runner_host_deploy, converged here, read back on the machine, and granted as the
// runner registration label gunbc.runner.runner_registration_labels gunbc_gpu_docker_label ONLY when
// the readback holds. Operator decision 2026-10-25 (node://adhoc-0cabf47d-be0): fleet CI jobs must
// run containers that see GPUs WITHOUT root equivalence; the S1b gate
// //gunbc/instruments:router-proxy-falsifier (#15089) and the serving benchmark both depend on it.
//
// WHY A CONVERGE AND NOT AN INSTALL. The brief's rule is that this is applied by `gunbc converge` and
// never hand-installed, so the only writer of the label and the only installer of the packages is the
// fold below, run from the fleet-converge workflow as mode runner_gpu_container_toolchain_converge.
// A host whose label was set by hand is a host that loses it at the next converge, because the label
// here is a READING of the machine and never a declaration of intent.
//
// WHY ROOTLESS, STATED TWICE BECAUSE IT IS THE WHOLE POINT. The capability is the JOB USER'S OWN
// daemon at unix:///run/user/<uid>/docker.sock — the endpoint
// gunbc.runner.runner_unit_file docker_host_from_uid_reading already forms for the slot — plus the
// NVIDIA container toolkit and a generated CDI spec. The rootful socket is NOT this capability:
// gunbc.fleet.fleet_posix_accounts fleet_root_equivalent_group_names classes "docker" as a
// root-equivalent group, so joining that group buys GPU containers by granting root, which is the
// thing the decision forbids. Readiness therefore asks whether the JOB USER can run a container that
// sees the devices; a host where only root can is not ready, however healthy its nvidia-smi.
//
// WHY THE READBACK RUNS FOUR ARMS AND NOT ONE. `--device nvidia.com/gpu=all` resolves only against a
// CDI spec, so a host with the packages and no /etc/cdi/nvidia.yaml fails at run time in a way no
// package query predicts; and a host with all three still fails if the job user's own daemon is not
// running (lingering off, socket gone). So the arms are: the toolkit package present, a CDI spec
// naming the complete-device arm, the job user's daemon answering, and — the one that actually
// decides — a container run by the job user through that daemon that sees a device. Each is a
// separate observed fact with its own refusal text, because "docker is installed" is not the question
// the two gates ask.
//
// MIRRORS, EXPLICITLY: gunbc.runner.runner_browser_toolchain (its apt ensure over the admin edge via
// gunbc.host.host_effect_realize srv3_ensure_apt, its typed-argv label drop-in, its *_ci_wet receipt
// entry). Deviations are stated where they occur, not hidden.

